-- Passage rg5-argument-evidence-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e1cc7c38-cd05-57a1-a30c-2f08d06954e6', 'rg5-argument-evidence-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$How the Canal Changed Harlow Falls$q$, $q$In 1820, Harlow Falls was a tiny village of about 300 people. Most families farmed, and the nearest big city was five days away by wagon. Twenty years later, the village had become a busy town. The reason, I believe, was the Harlow Canal.

First, the canal made shipping much cheaper. Before it opened in 1825, farmers paid high prices to send grain by wagon over rough roads. A canal boat pulled by mules could carry as much as forty wagons. According to the town's old shipping records, the cost of sending a barrel of flour to the city fell to about one tenth of what it had been.

Second, the canal brought new people. Workers came to dig the channel, and many of them stayed. Shopkeepers, blacksmiths and boat builders followed. By 1840, the town census counted more than 2,000 residents.

Third, the canal brought new ideas. Boats carried newspapers, books and letters from the city in just two days. The town opened its first library in 1832, stocked with books that arrived by boat.

Some historians point out that the railroad, which arrived in 1851, also helped the town grow. That is true. But by then, Harlow Falls had already grown more than six times larger. The canal had done the hard work first.$q$, 'draft')
