   $q$The author uses the Hollis Elementary example to support two different points. Which two?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Share tables are simple to run, and they teach students to respect food.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"Share tables help hungry students, and germs do not have to be a problem.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"Share tables cut down on waste, and they help students who are hungry.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"mismatched-reason"},{"text":"Share tables are simple to run, and germs do not have to be a problem.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Share tables are simple to run, and germs do not have to be a problem.$q$, $q$The Hollis rules appear in the "simple to run" paragraph and again in the answer to the germ worry ("The Hollis rules solve that problem"). The waste and hunger points rely on the class count and the nurse instead.$q$, 'draft', 'ded3b7fb-a490-5d40-ac8b-083f907f54a1')
